<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:120%;">Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VLM Performance on Different Patterns.</span> 
<a href="#S4.T2" title='In RAG-based inference on Failure Cases ‣ Mitigation framework: 𝖱𝖤𝖠𝖢𝖳⁢"-"⁢𝖣𝗋𝗂𝗏𝖾 ‣ Work Zones challenge VLM Trajectory Planning: Toward Mitigation and Robust Autonomous Driving' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the performance of different VLMs across <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> patterns on the ROADWork dataset, which shows that all <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> models perform unsatisfactorily in work zones.
The prediction errors remain high, with overall ADE around <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="192.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>192.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">192.15</annotation></semantics></math>, FDE close to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="371.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>371.94</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">371.94</annotation></semantics></math>, and average collision risk CR about <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.09" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.09</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.09</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results indicate that existing VLMs struggle to achieve reliable trajectory prediction and safe avoidance in the complex environments of work zones.
Among these VLMs, GPT4o achieves the best overall performance with the lowest average FDE of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="268.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>268.64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">268.64</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast, Gemini2.5 performs the worst overall, with an average FDE as high as <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="539.44" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>539.44</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">539.44</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating severe long-horizon errors.
Across work zone patterns, difficulty varies notably.
P2 and P8 are the hardest cases: P2 has an average FDE of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="550.12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>550.12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">550.12</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting long-term convergence errors; P8 reaches <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="514.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>514.07</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">514.07</annotation></semantics></math>, showing turning in work zones carries the greatest prediction risk;
Overall, current VLMs exhibit clear limitations in work zone environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mitigation Effectiveness and Performance Factors.</span> 
<a href="#S5.T3" title="In Experimental Setup ‣ Evaluation ‣ Work Zones challenge VLM Trajectory Planning: Toward Mitigation and Robust Autonomous Driving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> displays the average performance across the three mitigation settings.
We observe that <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{REACT\text{-}Drive}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖤𝖠𝖢𝖳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖣𝗋𝗂𝗏𝖾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{REACT\text{-}Drive}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the best overall results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of VLMs on mitigation tasks using <math id="S5.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{REACT\text{-}Drive}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖤𝖠𝖢𝖳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖣𝗋𝗂𝗏𝖾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{REACT\text{-}Drive}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T4.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:207.0pt;height:45pt;vertical-align:-19.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(17.8pt,-3.9pt) scale(1.20818202585115,1.20818202585115) ;">
<table id="S5.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">ADE <math id="S5.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">FDE <math id="S5.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">CR <math id="S5.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">GPT4o</td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">54.73</td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">101.64</td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.04</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Qwen2.5</td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">86.46</td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">124.67</td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.07</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02803v1/infer_times.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/200;" width="476" height="200"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Inference time comparison across different methods.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The fine-tuned version yields an ADE of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="207.97" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>207.97</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">207.97</annotation></semantics></math>, FDE of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="384.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>384.31</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">384.31</annotation></semantics></math>, and CR of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.11</annotation></semantics></math>.
The self-reasoning VLM without constraint rules further reduces the ADE and FDE to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="201.09" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>201.09</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">201.09</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="350.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>350.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">350.75</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, with CR dropping to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.03</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.03</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{REACT\text{-}Drive}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖤𝖠𝖢𝖳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖣𝗋𝗂𝗏𝖾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{REACT\text{-}Drive}</annotation></semantics></math> significantly lowers the ADE and FDE to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="54.73" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>54.73</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">54.73</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="101.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>101.64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">101.64</annotation></semantics></math>, while keeping CR at a relatively low level of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.04</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.04</annotation></semantics></math>.
In terms of pattern-specific mitigation, our method shows the most notable improvements in P1, P3, P6, and P7, where the collision risk is reduced to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.00</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.00</annotation></semantics></math> in all four cases.
In contrast, P4 and P8 show slightly increased CR,
but their FDE is significantly reduced.
This is because these two patterns sometimes occur in turning scenarios.
Please refer to the examples of P5 and P8 in <a href="#A6.F1" title="In Appendix F Details of Patterns ‣ Work Zones challenge VLM Trajectory Planning: Toward Mitigation and Robust Autonomous Driving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>. When the destination is correctly output, the trajectory may inevitably be close to work zone objects.
Furthermore, we investigate whether different VLM models will influence the mitigation effectiveness.
<a href="#S5.T4" title="In Experimental Results ‣ Evaluation ‣ Work Zones challenge VLM Trajectory Planning: Toward Mitigation and Robust Autonomous Driving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports the comparison between GPT4o and Qwen2.5 on mitigation tasks.
Overall, GPT4o demonstrates stronger performance across all three metrics.
Specifically, it achieves an ADE of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="54.73" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>54.73</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">54.73</annotation></semantics></math>, FDE of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="101.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>101.64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">101.64</annotation></semantics></math>, and CR of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.04</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.04</annotation></semantics></math>, which are consistently lower than Qwen2.5.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency.</span> 
<a href="#S5.F3" title="In Experimental Results ‣ Evaluation ‣ Work Zones challenge VLM Trajectory Planning: Toward Mitigation and Robust Autonomous Driving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compares inference time across different baselines.
Our method achieves the lowest latency (<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>s per scene), substantially faster than all other approaches.
In contrast, fine-tuning large-scale VLMs incurs significant overhead (<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 18</annotation></semantics></math>s), making them impractical for real-time deployment.
Other baselines such as GPT4o and RoboTron-Drive show moderate latency (<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 5</annotation></semantics></math>s and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 3</annotation></semantics></math>s), while SimLingo maintains relatively low cost but is still slower than our constraint-enhanced design.
These results highlight that our approach not only improves planning robustness in work zones but also provides clear efficiency advantages.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02803v1/pattern_coverage_lines_bold.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/229;" width="381" height="229"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pattern coverage experiments.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Physical experiment results.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T5.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:276.0pt;height:89.7pt;vertical-align:-41.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(25.4pt,-8.2pt) scale(1.22538360295198,1.22538360295198) ;">
<table id="S5.T5.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T5.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T5.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">ADE <math id="S5.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T5.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">FDE <math id="S5.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T5.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">CR <math id="S5.T5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
